<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>





<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 14 Practice</title>

    <p >
        <em>Negate a statement with and, or, not, implies,
                            exists, and/or for all.
        </em>
    </p>

   <paragraphs>
           <title>Need to know</title>
   
           <p>
               <ul>
                   <li>
                       <p>
                           How to negate conditional statements (e.g.
                           <q>if...then...</q> statements).
                       </p>
                   </li>
   
                   <li>
                       <p>
                           Use DeMorgan's Laws to negate statements with ``and'' and
                           ``or''
                       </p>
                   </li>
   
                   <li>
                       <p>
                           Negate statements that have ``there exists'' or ``for all''.
                       </p>
                   </li>
   
                   <li>
                       <p>
                           How to avoid the word not.
                           Like changing ``<m>n</m> is not even'' to ``<m>n</m> is
                           odd''.
                       </p>
                   </li>
               </ul>
           </p>
        </paragraphs>
   
       <paragraphs>
           <title>Practice Questions</title>
   
          <exercise>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Weekly Practice 10 #4
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         Worksheet pages 69-71
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                      Write a useful negation of the following statements.
                           Useful negations don't start with things like ``It is not
                           true that...'' and avoid the word not when possible.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>There exists <m>x\in\mathbb{Z}</m> such that if
                                       <m>y\in\mathbb{Z}</m> then
                                       <m>\dfrac{y}{x}\in \mathbb{Z}</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        For all <m>x\in \mathbb{Z}</m>, there exists  <m>y\in\mathbb{Z}</m> 
                        such that
                        <m>\dfrac{y}{x} \notin \mathbb{Z}</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        There's a <q>hidden quantifier</q> here (the hypothesis is <q>for all</q> <m>y\in\mathbb{Z}</m>).
                        So this statement is more readable with a <q>there exists.</q> For the learning target it is acceptable
                        to write <q>For all <m>x\in\mathbb{Z}</m>, <m>y\in\mathbb{Z} </m> and <m> \frac{y}{x} \notin\mathbb{Z}</m>.</q>
                        Do notice that our negation either way does not include an if and a then though!
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         For all <m>y \in \mathbb{R}</m> if <m>y\ge 0</m>
                                       then <m>\sqrt{y}\in \mathbb{R}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        There exists <m>y\in\mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>y\ge 0</m> and <m>\sqrt{y} \notin \mathbb{R}</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For all <m>r\in\mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>r&gt;2</m>
                                       then <m>r^{2}&gt;4</m>. 
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                       There exists <m>r\in \mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>r&gt; 2</m> and  <m>r^2 \leq 4</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Be careful with your inequailities here!
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         For every student in the class, if they studied
                                       and completed the homework, then they passed the
                                       exam.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        There exists a student in the class that studied and completed the 
                        homework and did not pass the exam.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         For every real number <m>x</m>, there exists a
                                       real number <m>y</m> such that <m>x + y = 0</m>. 
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        There exists a real number <m>x</m> such that for every real number <m>y</m>, <m>x+y\neq 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
      
   
                  
                         

             </exercise>
       </paragraphs>

        </handout>